                    \\tau_X &amp;\overset{\widetilde{(-)}}{\longrightarrow}&amp; \tau_{S X}
                    \\\
                    U &amp;\mapsto&amp; \tilde U
                    }
            %p
              in fact preserves arbitrary unions and finite intersections. Whith this the statement follows by the fact that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi τ
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau_X
              is closed under these operations.
            %p
              To see that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widetilde{(-)}
              indeed preserves unions, observe that (e.g.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#Johnstone82"} Johnstone 82, II 1.3 Lemma
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi p
                            %mo ∈
                            %munder
                              %mo ∪
                              %mrow
                                %mi i
                                %mo ∈
                                %mi I
                            %mover
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi U
                                  %mi i
                              %mo ˜
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⇔
                            %munder
                              %mo ∃
                              %mrow
                                %mi i
                                %mo ∈
                                %mi I
                            %mi p
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mi U
                              %mi i
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo =
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                            %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⇔
                            %munder
                              %mo ∪
                              %mrow
                                %mi i
